  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 AM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\n- Dry Conditions Continue, Fog Possible Friday Morning\n\nThe low pressure system currently over northeastern North Dakota\nwill advect a couple boundaries through the area as it lifts\nnortheast. Moisture isn't great, so just expecting some periods of\nhigh clouds. The low level jet associated with this feature will\nbring some breezy conditions today with gusts around 30 mph along\nand north of I-96 with gusts around 20 mph along I-94.\n\nHigh pressure builds in tonight with winds quickly becoming light \nand an inversion building in. Sounding profiles show dew point \ndepressions decreasing with low levels saturating. This is even \npresent in the GFS soundings. Looking at REFS probabilities, areas\nalong and south of I-96 have the best chances (greater than 50 \npercent) of visibilities less than 1 mile. \n\n- Rain Chances Increase Saturday Night into Sunday\n\nRain is likely late Saturday night into Sunday as a strong cold\nfront advances through the area. The right entrance region of\nthe upper level jet, positive vorticity advection, along with the\nlow level jet will bring sufficient lift ahead of the front.\nMoisture will also be advecting in from the south. Instability\nhowever is low, therefore not expecting any lightning.\nProbabilities of a quarter of an inch or more of rain are highest\n(50 to 70 percent) along and west of US-131. \n\nCold air moves in behind the front Sunday and may bring additional\nlake effect showers to the lakeshore Monday. 850mb and water\nsurface temperature differences are around 18 to 20 C, but the\ndepth of these showers will be shallow around 5000 feet, which may\nor may not be deep enough to introduce ice into the cloud layer \nfor snow.\n\n- Colder Next Week\n\nTemperatures next week will be much cooler with 850mb temperatures\nremaining below 0C. Long range ensemble members are in agreement\nthat highs will largely be in the 30s with lows in the 20s.",
